# codex-config.toml.schema
#
# Companion document for `codex-config.toml`, the Codex harness's configuration
# template. The build substitutes the `@…@` placeholders and writes the result to
# `<P-9 HARNESS_HOME>/config.toml`, which is the file this CLI reads (see
# `modules/router-wiring.md`).
#
# ## Every key, and why it is here
#
# | Key | Parameter | Effect |
# |---|---|---|
# | `model` | `P-5` | The model the session uses — an alias from the router's set. |
# | `model_provider` | — | Which provider block to use. The built-in `openai` provider is reserved and cannot be overridden, so pointing at the router is not the same as pointing at `openai` with a different URL. |
# | `model_context_window` | `P-7` | The context window available to the active model. A **top-level** key, next to `model` — not a field inside the provider block, which is where a reader would expect it. |
# | `model_providers.router.name` | — | A display name for the provider block. |
# | `model_providers.router.base_url` | `P-3` | The router's API base URL. |
# | `model_providers.router.env_key` | `P-4` | The **name** of the environment variable the credential arrives in. This is why the file names a credential without carrying one. |
# | `model_providers.router.wire_api` | — | The protocol used to reach the provider. `responses` is the only supported value. |
#
# ## The condition this file places on the router
#
# Codex CLI speaks the Responses API. A router that serves chat completions alone
# will accept this configuration and fail at the first request, so
# `wire_api = "responses"` is a compatibility requirement on the endpoint
# (the dependency's failure behaviour), not a tunable. Translating between the two
# protocols is a second service, which this layer must not add (R-2).
#
# ## The field this CLI does not have
#
# There is no key for maximum output tokens. `P-8` is therefore unused for this
# harness, and that gap is stated rather than filled with a plausible-looking key
# that the CLI would ignore.
#
# ## What must not be in this file
#
# A credential value: `env_key` names the variable, and an inline bearer-token
# field or a token pasted into `base_url` would put a credential in an image layer
# (R-8). Nor a second provider block for a role the router serves (R-7).
#
# ## Validation
#
# ```
# docker run --rm --entrypoint sh <image> -c 'cat "${CODEX_HOME}/config.toml"'
# ```
#
# The file must parse as TOML (`@…@` left in place does not), carry no credential
# value, and name exactly one provider.
